#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100640 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba3edd0-9bbf-5d0f-aa0b-1a64cb465a77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Trigonometric Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "5d78daa9-ba7a-554d-83f7-f8354acb9976" :question/math-academy-id "e-5372" :question/problem "Find the values of $\\theta$ at which the curve $x=\\cos \\theta, \\, y = 2 \\sin \\theta, \\, 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$ intersects the line $2x-y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan(1) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\leq \\theta <     2\\pi,$ so our first solution is $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi$ to $\\theta_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + \\pi = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\,\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100641 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afc21191-a71b-5dac-8a4a-e46e8bc03a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "dc97ffbb-04ca-5b07-ba25-11042f15c8ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-5373" :question/problem "Find the points where the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/assets/ba682be1ab26bad257fe814020469696f5c302a765a67c45c341762ac9418108.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ Upon finding $t,$ we then proceed to find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y-15 & =0 \\\\ 4({t}^{2})-2(2t)-15 & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}-4t-15 & =0 \\\\ (2t-5)(2t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t,$ we proceed to find $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- We start with $t=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ x & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2t \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the first intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right).$\n\n- We now move over to $t=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ x & ={(-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2t \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the second intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0$  at the points $\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right)$ and $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afc21191-a71b-5dac-8a4a-e46e8bc03a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b21c27f-51c9-52c6-ba52-22f3f81339e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-6,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342100} {:answer/id #uuid "0454b450-2491-5a5c-a38d-93412a68152c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-5,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342101} {:answer/id #uuid "0289228c-8bd7-5a7b-b2ee-ae8a1f838f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-2,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342102} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2eaf3d-5f84-5873-953d-137916aafe03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-8,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342103} {:answer/id #uuid "65922077-5e4b-5397-b708-7d9156174391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-4,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65922077-5e4b-5397-b708-7d9156174391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-4,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342104} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a77f0d9-9b4a-5852-bab4-def96d99e2aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fafd9e8-7e2c-5da3-8224-387ebb42b609" :question/math-academy-id "q-102605" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/assets/dcac814ed1c1caf5a8796a25ccee57cc5fd988d10f24503cbf0755aeaedb5e2c.png)\n\nFind the values of $t$ at which the curve $x=8t,y=-{t}^{2}$ intersects the line $\\frac{1}{2}x-y=0,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of the intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8t)-(-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4t+{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(4+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba3edd0-9bbf-5d0f-aa0b-1a64cb465a77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Trigonometric Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cca5c6f9-9ecc-5b70-9979-e6c98222f49a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342107} {:answer/id #uuid "b813a7bc-5d32-5a20-a398-4cf85e83e27d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342108} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd57dc9-b855-5e9a-a6cc-c3de93d6fa06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342109} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a8564e-6ded-539c-b919-ba8b6e841361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342110} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ca16a8-1284-50f7-a316-3444027c2f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4ca16a8-1284-50f7-a316-3444027c2f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342111} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d108d3-928b-5080-b96f-b54581e22218" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74376924-ed55-5466-916c-abf65e0085f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-102633" :question/problem "Find the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $x-5y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5y & =0 \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afc21191-a71b-5dac-8a4a-e46e8bc03a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198441e9-2ce9-5e01-8be8-414085342ac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{1}{2},3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342114} {:answer/id #uuid "428faf37-52e0-523b-8347-6fa422602042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342115} {:answer/id #uuid "adc2d09d-0547-50d5-a512-65d1bdabff44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{1}{2},4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342116} {:answer/id #uuid "1f251872-a49d-50b1-9658-c8d736b6fa5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342117} {:answer/id #uuid "73cdaf68-24f2-5188-8dee-bc4ae5ecd42b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f251872-a49d-50b1-9658-c8d736b6fa5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342117} :answer-field/id #uuid "637c07bf-0caa-506e-9245-56965b8cfe76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e73f8f1-1fc2-5b0b-9784-8572e1ec7d5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-16166" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/assets/a1959f7344a33f2bd73f00f1ef999acd0d30767b25fa41fe39dc0e7a4bda612a.png)\n\nFind the values of $t$ at which the curve $x=-{t}^{2},y=-3t$ intersects the line $x-y+4=0,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of the intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+4 & =0 \\\\ (-{t}^{2})-(-3t)+4 & =0 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+3t+4 & =0 \\\\ -(t+1)(t-4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba3edd0-9bbf-5d0f-aa0b-1a64cb465a77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Trigonometric Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b46f4d0-8627-5b28-b4e7-7a8115e3c0b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342121} {:answer/id #uuid "76cb81ba-521b-5e15-9011-60778fdf52e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342122} {:answer/id #uuid "087c2bbd-ff5a-5c03-977e-db28acb32f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342123} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0cfbf3-a474-588a-a8f6-ee27ee7a5f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342124} {:answer/id #uuid "e39756fc-87b8-5d77-a1dd-db579bdb2113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "087c2bbd-ff5a-5c03-977e-db28acb32f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342123} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9cea939-28cb-56ad-b007-22bdaefef6a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50a3f4ad-674b-5669-b9c0-40f8b988aec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-16178" :question/problem "Find the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $\\sqrt{3}x+y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}x+y & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afc21191-a71b-5dac-8a4a-e46e8bc03a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "435ff245-ff47-5939-ae21-4ea4d0a92b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "435ff245-ff47-5939-ae21-4ea4d0a92b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342128} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab80bea9-201d-5742-aad6-fc6eeeb9bfc7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342127} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9297d38-5550-5b27-9cfb-335af5980727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9297d38-5550-5b27-9cfb-335af5980727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342130} :answer-field/id #uuid "24a0e711-94c0-58a4-a8ce-787bad8b43a7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a1e3d7b-489e-57fd-97e8-34f13b4e5257" :question/math-academy-id "q-274671" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/assets/3ec88af84d96caed2c27b9b1596b5320ff7ceaaac71ce12e48623c2be52f18ba.png)\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values of $t$ at which the curve $x=t+6,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-4$ intersects the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-1$ (shown above) are\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we expect two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of the intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-4 & =\\frac{1}{2}(t+6)-1 \\\\ {t}^{2}-8 & =(t+6)-2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-8 & =t+4 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-12 & =0 \\\\ (t+3)(t-4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $t$ where the curve $x=t+6,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-4$ intersects the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-1$ are $t=-3$ and $t=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afc21191-a71b-5dac-8a4a-e46e8bc03a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e97f438d-0294-501a-8248-3306ad8af745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342133} {:answer/id #uuid "2c57436f-bc5d-502c-96e6-6d12543d5e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e97f438d-0294-501a-8248-3306ad8af745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342133} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cddf78a-4b10-581b-b222-be57730f9a28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342132} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c41ba5df-2a19-532e-91b7-78663ce57225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c41ba5df-2a19-532e-91b7-78663ce57225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342136} :answer-field/id #uuid "eceeb209-5315-5811-bdba-28b78081f09b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342135} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "578388b3-345b-582e-b356-ee5c92988fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "578388b3-345b-582e-b356-ee5c92988fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342138} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb5bb6f7-0379-5573-9ff2-8bec978d727c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbb7e394-0cf1-5a5c-a310-9a9d31edc44a" :question/math-academy-id "q-274673" :question/problem "Find the points where the curve $x={t}^{2},\\,y=4t$ intersects the line $x+y+3=0.$\n\nThe intersection points are $(1,$\n{{field-1}}$)$ and $(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ Upon finding $t,$ we then find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+3 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}+4t+3 & =0 \\\\ (t+3)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t,$ we proceed to find $x$ and $y:$ \n\n- We start with $t=-3.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\  & =(-3)^{2} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4t \\\\  & =4(-3) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the first intersection point are $(9,-12).$\n\n- We now move over to $t=-1.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4t \\\\  & =4(-1) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the second intersection point are $(1,-4).$\n\nTherefore, the curve $x={t}^{2},y=4t$ intersects the line $x+y+3=0$ at the points $(1,-4)$ and $(9,-12).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afc21191-a71b-5dac-8a4a-e46e8bc03a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba4e6fa-0533-5f8f-9e17-ed9ac11a4680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342141} {:answer/id #uuid "23534205-d925-581b-819c-0b3a63a2bae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba4e6fa-0533-5f8f-9e17-ed9ac11a4680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342141} :answer-field/id #uuid "f787ab61-7e81-5e51-9a54-ae6936d25143" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342140} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8446a18d-94ed-5b15-bd3c-ac795ed67864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8446a18d-94ed-5b15-bd3c-ac795ed67864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342144} :answer-field/id #uuid "d583b822-be47-50ee-bddf-8202fc4166d5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342143} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58718824-7776-5b22-95a4-e86eb3885e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58718824-7776-5b22-95a4-e86eb3885e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342146} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b0eacc1-f9c6-5926-ab8f-4e60be0cefab" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "814af89f-6240-5b38-a4c8-caac7366210d" :question/math-academy-id "q-320686" :question/problem "Find the points where the curve $x=4t,\\,y=\\frac{32}{t}$ intersects the line $\\frac{1}{2}x-y=0.$\n\nThe intersection points are $(-16,$\n{{field-1}}$)$ and $(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ Upon finding $t,$ we then find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4t)-\\frac{32}{t} & =0 \\\\ 2t-\\frac{32}{t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2{t}^{2}-32}{t} & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-32 & =0 \\\\ 2({t}^{2}-16) & =0 \\\\ 2(t+4)(t-4) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t,$ we proceed to find $x$ and $y.$ \n\n- We start with $t=-4.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4t \\\\  & =4(-4) \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{32}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the first intersection point are $(-16,-8).$\n\n- We now move over to $t=4.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4t \\\\  & =4(4) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{32}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{4} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the second intersection point are $(16,8).$\n\nTherefore, the curve $x=4t,\\,y=\\frac{32}{t}$ intersects the line $\\frac{1}{2}x-y=0$ at the points $(-16,-8)$ and $(16,8).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba3edd0-9bbf-5d0f-aa0b-1a64cb465a77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Trigonometric Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf184d60-bd1c-57ca-9a3a-8381d419ad06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342149} {:answer/id #uuid "5bee9285-247c-5822-bfed-7822d6a248b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf184d60-bd1c-57ca-9a3a-8381d419ad06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342149} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dc3504f-61bb-5e8a-9082-7896820b72b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342148} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6bd22f5-9c8f-5fd6-b483-cd0264c966f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6bd22f5-9c8f-5fd6-b483-cd0264c966f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342152} :answer-field/id #uuid "c170e32b-02eb-503b-8a0d-9297e0ee75b8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e216f63e-6792-5778-92c0-9dae49201579" :question/math-academy-id "q-320696" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $2\\sqrt{3}\\,y=x$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\,y & =x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba3edd0-9bbf-5d0f-aa0b-1a64cb465a77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Trigonometric Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "901020d6-1243-5159-868a-53f40cac35a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342155} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1f35c5-624a-55f6-b2d3-b812c9298f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce1f35c5-624a-55f6-b2d3-b812c9298f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342156} :answer-field/id #uuid "577205e3-b3b3-51bc-9a82-c1fb625e405b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342154} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4663ef2d-ba6c-5580-99e0-e19be4ff7414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4663ef2d-ba6c-5580-99e0-e19be4ff7414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342158} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15d0a15-084f-5fc7-a102-216c7bb1ddc8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f50a531-00f6-5973-a263-cf574ee4f075" :question/math-academy-id "q-320705" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $y+2\\sqrt{3}\\,x=0$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2\\sqrt{3}\\,x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =arctan\\,(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba3edd0-9bbf-5d0f-aa0b-1a64cb465a77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Trigonometric Parametric Curve and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e2988ea-9a75-5853-b00e-c6e0cbc77514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e2988ea-9a75-5853-b00e-c6e0cbc77514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342161} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd3b5dbe-3135-5f08-a051-0124a9af6380" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342160} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179d4564-b03d-5d49-a0b3-2a52ac0eab02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "179d4564-b03d-5d49-a0b3-2a52ac0eab02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342163} :answer-field/id #uuid "85be5aad-d193-5242-aec3-d413f016943e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "257853a6-f390-514e-8b43-63ff1af6b27b" :question/math-academy-id "q-320707" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $3y+x=0$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+x & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$"}]}
